          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Education and History</strong><br>\nHerder's work had significant implications for the philosophy of education and history. He emphasized the importance of education in developing a sense of cultural identity and understanding human history as a tapestry of these unique identities.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Pastoral Poetry</strong><br>\nIn later years, Herder turned to writing pastoral poetry that reflected his ideas about natural language and cultural expression. His poems often celebrated the virtues of rural life and the wisdom of the common people.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nHerder died in 1803, but his ideas have had a profound and lasting influence. His works are considered seminal in the study of history, culture, and language, deeply affecting fields as diverse as theology and literary criticism.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
